# tv-helper environment. Copy to `.env.local` (gitignored) and fill in.
#
# EVERY VARIABLE THE APP READS IS LISTED HERE, so a fresh clone never has to
# grep the source to find out what it must supply. Required ones are present
# and empty, except AUTH_URL, which is filled in because the correct local
# value is knowable in advance and is the same for everyone. Optional ones are
# COMMENTED OUT, and that is deliberate rather than untidy -- see the warning
# immediately below.
#
# Cross-check against the source with:
#   grep -rhoE 'process\.env\.[A-Z_0-9]+' src/ scripts/ | sort -u
# Two names the app depends on will NOT appear in that list, because nothing in
# this repo reads them directly: AUTH_URL is read by Auth.js itself, and PORT /
# HOSTNAME by the standalone server Next generates.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THE OPTIONAL VARIABLES ARE COMMENTED OUT AND MUST STAY THAT WAY
#
# Every default in this app is applied with `??`, which falls back on
# `undefined` but NOT on the empty string. An unset variable is `undefined`
# and gets the default; a variable written as `NAME=` is `""` and is used as
# if you had chosen it. So uncommenting `GEMINI_THINKING_LEVEL=` and leaving
# it blank does not mean "use the default", it sends an empty thinking level
# to Gemini. The same trap applies to MODEL_ID and GEMINI_MEDIA_RESOLUTION.
#
# Uncomment a line only when you are also giving it a value.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# === REQUIRED: inference =====================================================

# Gemini API key from https://aistudio.google.com/apikey. The app has no local
# fallback and `/api/chat` and `/api/propose` return 503 without it.
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY -- never give this a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, which would ship
# it in the browser bundle. Read only by src/lib/model.ts.
GOOGLE_GENERATIVE_AI_API_KEY=


# === REQUIRED: sign-in =======================================================
#
# Without these, `pnpm dev` serves a sign-in page that cannot complete: the
# button is there, the OAuth redirect fails, and you land back on /signin with
# ?error=Configuration. That is the intended failure -- there is no anonymous
# mode outside the one-shot bootstrap deploy in docs/runbook-deploy.md -- but
# it looks like a bug if you were not expecting it. `pnpm test`, `pnpm build`,
# `pnpm generate` and `pnpm smoke` need none of it.

# Signs the session JWT. Any high-entropy string; `openssl rand -base64 32` or
# `npx auth secret`. Changing it signs everyone out.
AUTH_SECRET=

# Google OAuth Web application client, from Google Cloud console ->
# APIs & Services -> Credentials. For local work create a SEPARATE client whose
# authorized redirect URI is exactly:
#   http://localhost:3000/api/auth/callback/google
# Setting AUTH_GOOGLE_ID is also the interlock that permanently disables
# AUTH_DISABLED, so a forgotten bootstrap flag cannot un-gate a real install.
AUTH_GOOGLE_ID=
AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET=

# The canonical URL this app is reached at; Auth.js builds the OAuth callback
# from it. `trustHost: true` derives it from forwarded headers if unset, which
# works but leaves the callback at the mercy of whatever proxy is in front.
# On Cloud Run set it to the service URL. Locally it is the dev server.
AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3000


# === OPTIONAL: everything below has a working default ========================
#
# Re-read the warning at the top before uncommenting any of these: a blank
# value is a chosen value, not a default.

# The model. Must be vision-capable, since reading scans is the product.
# Chosen by measurement, not by version number: gemini-3.7-flash took 99-190s
# on a trivial vision call with intermittent 503s, past /api/chat's 120s
# maxDuration. Run `pnpm smoke` before moving it.
#
# THAT WAS A LATENCY DECISION AND NEVER A COST ONE, and it happens to have
# pinned the most expensive Flash tier on the price list: gemini-3.5-flash is
# $1.50/M input and $9.00/M output, against $0.75/$3.75 for 3.6/3.7/3.8-flash
# and $0.30/$2.50 for 3.5-flash-lite. See src/lib/cost.ts for the table and
# `pnpm generate`'s per-stage cost report for what a run actually spends.
# MODEL_ID=gemini-3.5-flash

# WHICH MODEL READS THE SCANS, separately from the one that reasons about them.
# Defaults to MODEL_ID, so unset it changes nothing.
#
# This exists because the two jobs have opposite cost profiles. OCR is the only
# call whose legitimate reply is long -- every text block on a 300 DPI page
# with its box -- so it is billed almost entirely on OUTPUT, where the pinned
# model charges $9.00/M against flash-lite's $2.50/M. The reasoning stages are
# billed on INPUT and are the judgements a validator signs, so they are the
# wrong place to economise. Measured on the sample bundle, OCR is roughly half
# the bill and this variable is the single largest lever on it.
#
# It is part of both scripts' OCR cache keys, so switching it re-reads the
# bundle rather than scoring a new model's answers against old page text.
# Verify (src/lib/pipeline/verify.ts) deliberately ignores it and stays on
# MODEL_ID: that pass exists to catch the whole-page read confabulating small
# print, so running it on the cheaper model would weaken the guard against the
# cheaper model's likeliest mistake.
#
# Change it only with `pnpm measure:locate` re-run and the per-page OCR table
# compared -- `node scripts/compare-ocr.mjs <baseline.txt> <candidate.txt>`.
# The gate's headline score cannot judge this on its own: three identical runs
# measured 11/12, 9/12 and 11/12, so a one-slot difference is inside the noise.
# OCR_MODEL_ID=gemini-3.5-flash-lite

# Cost levers, measured per image and per request; the table is in AGENTS.md.
# LOW 274 | MEDIUM 528 | HIGH 1110 prompt tokens per image, a FLAT RATE per
# tier regardless of pixel size. HIGH is the default because this project
# exists to read small print off scans; MEDIUM halves input cost by discarding
# exactly the detail that decides a verdict. Change it only with accuracy
# measured on real documents.
# GEMINI_MEDIA_RESOLUTION=MEDIA_RESOLUTION_HIGH

# minimal 0 | low 40 | medium 194 | high 324 thought tokens, billed at the
# OUTPUT rate. "low" is the default and already saves ~79% against Gemini's
# own default of medium with no measured loss on field extraction.
# GEMINI_THINKING_LEVEL=low

# Runaway-generation guard, not a budget: the model will otherwise emit up to
# 65536. A reply cut short logs a warning naming this variable.
# GEMINI_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=4096

# The OCR stage's own cap, separate on purpose. OCR is the one call whose
# legitimate reply is long -- every text block on a 300 DPI page with its box,
# measured at 2554 output tokens on a dense contract page -- and raising the
# 4096 guard above to suit it would remove a real protection from every other
# call. A truncated line list is a silently short page, so the OCR callers
# throw rather than warn when a reply hits this.
# GEMINI_OCR_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=16384

# Which engine reads the scans, for the two SCRIPTS only -- `pnpm generate` and
# `pnpm measure:locate`. "tesseract" (the default) recognises on this machine
# and needs no credential; "gemini" sends one rendered page image per page to
# the model. The browser deliberately has no such switch: a runtime engine
# choice there would let two geometry sources mix inside one bundle, and its
# kill switch is reverting the commit instead.
#
# THIS IS THE ONLY WAY TO RUN THE A/B the whole measurement sequence is built
# on, so run the gate once per engine and compare, never against a transcript.
# The engine, the model id and the OCR prompt version are all part of the OCR
# cache key, so a switch cannot serve the other engine's text at the new
# engine's speed.
# OCR_ENGINE=tesseract

# How many pages `pnpm generate` OCRs at once. Defaults to 4 under Gemini,
# where the wait is a network round trip, and 1 under tesseract, which is
# already using the CPU. Each page in flight holds a 300 DPI RGBA bitmap
# (~33MB), so this is the peak-memory lever: 4 is ~140MB, and the whole
# 29-page bundle at once would be about a gigabyte.
# OCR_CONCURRENCY=4

# The crop-level second pass, which re-reads every value bound for xlsx column
# E from a crop of its own cited region and BLANKS THE CELL on disagreement
# rather than picking a winner. It exists because the model transcribes small
# print confidently, deterministically and invisibly wrong at whole-page
# resolution while reading the same region perfectly as a crop.
#
# Set to 0 only for a controlled A/B, never for speed: it is ~15 small calls a
# run, and the failure it catches is a plausible wrong value in a cell a human
# validator signs. It verifies VALUES, not CROPS -- only `pnpm measure:locate`
# catches a picture of the wrong region.
# GENERATE_VERIFY=1

# Firestore holds the allowlist and is reached through Application Default
# Credentials -- no key file, nothing in src/lib/auth/firestore.ts reads a
# credential itself. Locally that means `gcloud auth application-default
# login`; without it every lookup returns lookup-failed and only the hardcoded
# bootstrap owner gets in, which is a fair simulation of an outage.
# ALLOWLIST_COLLECTION=allowlist
# ALLOWLIST_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Serves every request WITHOUT authentication. Honoured only while
# AUTH_GOOGLE_ID is empty, and only for the exact string "true". This exists
# for one thing: the first Cloud Run deploy, which has to mint the service URL
# that the OAuth client's redirect URI needs (docs/runbook-deploy.md, Trap 1).
# Do not use it to skip local setup.
# AUTH_DISABLED=true

# Read by src/proxy.ts only as a fallback for AUTH_SECRET, for a tree carrying
# next-auth v4 configuration. Set AUTH_SECRET instead; this is listed so the
# name is not a surprise when you meet it in the source.
# NEXTAUTH_SECRET=

# The port and bind address the server listens on. The Dockerfile sets both
# (8080 and 0.0.0.0) because Cloud Run requires them; `next dev` defaults to
# 3000 on localhost and needs neither.
# PORT=3000
# HOSTNAME=0.0.0.0

# NODE_ENV is deliberately NOT settable here. `src/lib/ui/stub-runtime.ts`
# reads it to refuse to construct in a production build, but the bundler
# INLINES its value at build time, so a line in this file would change nothing
# in the browser and could only mislead. `next dev` sets development and
# `next build` sets production; the Dockerfile sets it for the server process.


# === OPTIONAL: read only by scripts, never by the app ========================

# `pnpm smoke` calls the native Gemini REST surface directly. Override only to
# point the smoke test somewhere else; the app ignores this entirely.
# GEMINI_BASE_URL=https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta

# `pnpm generate` (scripts/generate.mjs). FORCE=1 bypasses the on-disk OCR
# cache AND, when the reply cache below is on, refreshes its entries too.
# TIMEOUT_MS is the ceiling on ONE model request (default 180000); an abort
# counts as transient and costs a retry rather than the run.
# GENERATE_FORCE=1
# GENERATE_TIMEOUT_MS=180000

# CACHE MODEL REPLIES TO DISK. A development affordance, off by default, and
# the default is the point: AGENTS.md's rule stands that a stale verdict served
# silently is worse than paying again, so this is never on in anything whose
# output a human reads as fresh.
#
# What it buys is the re-run loop. Iterating on the exporters, the geometry or
# the reports re-asks the same seven locate questions about the same unchanged
# pages every time, at about $0.27 a run, and every one of those answers was
# already paid for. The cache key carries the model id, the thinking level and
# the output cap, so changing any of them misses by construction instead of
# serving the previous model's answers under the new model's banner.
#
# A run that served anything from this cache says so loudly in its summary,
# because a cached run is not a measurement.
# GENERATE_CACHE_MODEL=1

# Rupiah per dollar, for the second column of `pnpm generate`'s cost table.
# A reading convenience, not a rate this repo warrants: every vendor price is
# in dollars and the invoice that prompted the cost work arrived in rupiah.
# Printed alongside the figure so a reader can see which rate produced it.
# IDR_PER_USD=16500

# `pnpm measure:locate` (scripts/measure-locate.mjs). FORCE=1 bypasses the
# model-reply cache and only that one, never the OCR caches. ONLY is a
# case-insensitive substring of a slot name, to score one slot in isolation.
# REPEAT is a non-negative integer that re-asks the same prompt under a
# separate cache key, to measure the run-to-run noise floor.
# MEASURE_LOCATE_FORCE=1
# MEASURE_LOCATE_ONLY=
# MEASURE_LOCATE_REPEAT=0
